# Line endings, so that a contributor on Linux and a contributor on Windows see the same
# bytes in a diff.
#
# **Why this file exists.** The repository held 457 files with LF endings and 186 with CRLF —
# 97 Python files, 22 Markdown, 19 `.mjs`, 30 `.ts`/`.tsx` among them. Nothing broke, because
# every tool here tolerates both. What broke was review: a one-line change to a CRLF file
# arrived as a whole-file diff on a machine that checked out LF, and a whole-file diff is a
# diff nobody reads. Found by counting, not by noticing.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF **in the repository** and lets each platform check out what it
# asks for (`core.eol` / `core.autocrlf`). It does not force LF into anyone's working tree,
# which is why it is used here instead of `eol=lf`: this is a Windows-first product and a
# Windows checkout is entitled to CRLF.
* text=auto

# **These keep CRLF on every platform.** `cmd.exe` reads a batch file as it goes and treats
# labels as positions, so an LF-only `.bat` can jump somewhere unexpected; PowerShell's parser
# has its own rules about line breaks. All of these run on Windows and nowhere else, so there
# is no platform to be neutral towards.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary. `text=auto` would usually detect these, but a PNG that happens to contain the bytes
# `\r\n` is exactly the case where "usually" is not good enough — and that happened here.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.7z binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.pyd binary
*.so binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary

# Generated, and a diff on it is noise — but it must still be reviewed when it changes,
# because that is what pins the dependency tree.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
